from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"When you flex your elbow to lift a glass, the muscle acting as the prime mover (agonist) is the...","options":["triceps","biceps","quadriceps","diaphragm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The biceps on the front of the upper arm shortens to flex the elbow, so it is the agonist."},{"prompt":"A tendon connects...","options":["bone to bone","muscle to bone","muscle to muscle","nerve to muscle"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A tendon joins muscle to bone; a ligament joins bone to bone."},{"prompt":"Most of the oxygen in blood travels attached to...","options":["plasma","hemoglobin","platelets","white blood cells"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hemoglobin inside red blood cells carries the large majority of the oxygen."},{"prompt":"A single maximal effort lasting about two seconds is powered mainly by...","options":["the aerobic system","anaerobic glycolysis","the ATP-PC system","fat oxidation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The ATP-PC system uses stored phosphocreatine to recharge ATP almost instantly for very short bursts."},{"prompt":"Increasing the training demand gradually over weeks is the principle of...","options":["reversibility","progression","specificity","balance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Progression means raising the demand bit by bit so overload continues as the body adapts."},{"prompt":"The main fuel stored as glycogen for hard exercise is...","options":["protein","fat","carbohydrate","vitamins"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Carbohydrate is stored as glycogen in muscle and liver and is the main fuel for hard effort."},{"prompt":"When you push down on the ground to jump, the ground pushes back with an equal and opposite...","options":["gravity","ground reaction force","air resistance","friction only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The ground reaction force is the equal and opposite push described by Newton''s third idea of motion."},{"prompt":"Regular physical activity lowers the risk of...","options":["type 2 diabetes and heart disease","all injuries entirely","needing any sleep","growing taller"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Activity reduces the risk of conditions such as type 2 diabetes and heart disease, among other benefits."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'The musculoskeletal system and movement', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to name the main components of the musculoskeletal system - bones, joints, skeletal muscles, tendons, and ligaments - and explain how a contracting muscle produces movement at a joint. You will describe the roles muscles play as agonist, antagonist, and fixator, and you will use the idea of a lever to see why some movements are fast while others are strong. You will also be able to tell the main muscle fiber types apart and say why a sprinter and a marathon runner rely on different mixes of them. Warm-up from the diagnostic: when you bend your elbow to raise a cup toward your mouth, the biceps on the front of your upper arm shortens to create the movement while the triceps on the back lengthens to allow it. Think about which muscle would shorten instead if you were straightening the elbow to set the cup back down on the table."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The skeleton is a frame of bones meeting at joints. Skeletal muscles attach to bones by tough cords called tendons, while ligaments tie bone to bone and keep joints stable. A muscle can only pull, never push, so muscles are arranged in opposing pairs. The muscle that shortens to drive a movement is the agonist, or prime mover; the muscle on the other side that relaxes and lengthens is the antagonist. Inside a muscle, tiny units called sarcomeres shorten when filaments of actin and myosin slide past one another, a process powered by energy from food and triggered by signals from nerves. A joint plus the muscles around it acts as a lever: the joint is the pivot, the bone is the rigid arm, and the muscle supplies the effort. Because the muscle usually attaches close to the joint, it must generate a large force to move a load held far out along the limb, which trades strength for speed and range. Skeletal muscle also contains a mix of slow fibers, built for endurance, and fast fibers, built for short bursts of power."},{"heading":"Key methods & examples","text":"To analyze a movement, first name the joint and the action, then find the agonist and antagonist. Example: standing up from a squat straightens the knee, so the quadriceps on the front of the thigh is the agonist and the hamstrings on the back are the antagonist. Example: a biceps curl flexes the elbow, so the biceps is the agonist and the triceps is the antagonist; reverse the roles when you lower the weight slowly, because the biceps now lengthens under tension to control the load. The lever idea explains sports technique. A long tennis racket extends the lever arm so the racket head moves fast, turning a small shoulder rotation into a quick swing. Fiber type explains specialization: a 100 meter sprinter carries a high share of fast fibers that contract quickly and fatigue fast, while a marathon runner relies on slow fibers that resist fatigue and burn oxygen steadily. Posture needs fixator muscles too; when you curl a weight, muscles of the trunk and shoulder hold the upper arm still so the movement stays clean. Naming joint, action, agonist, and antagonist in that order gives a reliable recipe for any movement."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) When you straighten your elbow to lower a cup, which muscle is the agonist? 2) What connective tissue joins muscle to bone? 3) Name the prime mover when you stand up out of a chair. 4) Why can a muscle pull but not push? 5) Which fiber type dominates in a marathon specialist? 6) In a lever, what role does the joint play? Answers: 1) The triceps shortens to extend the elbow, so it is the agonist, and the biceps is the antagonist. 2) A tendon joins muscle to bone, while a ligament joins bone to bone. 3) The quadriceps extends the knee and is the prime mover, helped by the gluteal muscles at the hip. 4) Muscle fibers can only contract and shorten, so to move a bone back the opposing muscle must pull it the other way. 5) Slow fibers, which resist fatigue and rely on a steady oxygen supply, dominate in endurance athletes. 6) The joint is the pivot, or fulcrum, around which the rigid bone turns when the muscle supplies effort."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: calling every muscle on the moving side the agonist. In any single action only the muscle driving that action is the agonist, and its partner relaxes as the antagonist; the roles switch when the movement reverses. Mistake: thinking a muscle pushes a bone back into place. A muscle only pulls, so the return movement needs the opposing muscle of the pair. Mistake: assuming more fast fibers is always better. Fast fibers give power but tire quickly, which is wrong for endurance events. Exit ticket: (a) In an elbow curl lifting a weight, which muscle is the agonist and which is the antagonist? (b) What is the difference between a tendon and a ligament? Answers: (a) The biceps is the agonist because it shortens to flex the elbow, and the triceps is the antagonist because it lengthens to allow the movement. (b) A tendon attaches muscle to bone and transmits the pulling force, while a ligament attaches bone to bone and holds the joint together."}]}'::jsonb, 'A muscle can only pull, so name the agonist as the muscle that shortens and the antagonist as its lengthening partner.', 13),
  (3, 'The cardiovascular and respiratory systems', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"After this lesson you will be able to trace a drop of blood through the heart and lungs, explain how oxygen travels from the air you breathe to a working muscle, and describe how the heart and lungs respond when you start to exercise. You will define key measures such as heart rate, stroke volume, and cardiac output, and you will see why trained endurance athletes often have a low resting heart rate. You will also be able to explain the role of hemoglobin in carrying oxygen and why breathing rate climbs during hard effort. Warm-up from the diagnostic: your heart beats about seventy times a minute at rest, pushing out a certain volume of blood each beat. If both the beat rate and the volume per beat rise during exercise, what happens to the total amount of blood pumped each minute, and why would a muscle sprinting for the finish line need exactly that change?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The cardiovascular system is the heart, the blood, and the vessels. The right side of the heart sends oxygen-poor blood to the lungs; the left side pumps oxygen-rich blood to the body. Arteries carry blood away from the heart, veins return it, and tiny capillaries are where exchange happens. In the lungs, air reaches small sacs called alveoli, and oxygen crosses their thin walls into the blood while carbon dioxide passes out to be breathed away. Most oxygen rides on hemoglobin inside red blood cells. The respiratory system moves air in and out through the action of the diaphragm and the muscles between the ribs. Three useful numbers describe the pump. Heart rate is beats per minute. Stroke volume is the blood ejected per beat. Cardiac output is the product of the two, the blood delivered each minute. During exercise the body needs far more oxygen, so heart rate and stroke volume both rise and cardiac output can climb several times over. Breathing deepens and quickens to keep loading oxygen and clearing carbon dioxide. Endurance training strengthens the heart muscle so each beat pumps more blood, which lets the resting heart rate fall while cardiac output at rest stays the same."},{"heading":"Key methods & examples","text":"Cardiac output follows a simple formula: cardiac output equals heart rate times stroke volume. Example: at rest a heart beating 70 times a minute with a stroke volume of 70 milliliters pumps 70 times 70, about 4,900 milliliters, close to five liters a minute. During hard exercise heart rate might reach 180 and stroke volume 120, giving 180 times 120, about 21,600 milliliters, more than twenty liters a minute. Example: a trained cyclist with a strong heart may rest at a heart rate near 45 because a large stroke volume still delivers the needed five liters. To trace oxygen, follow the path: air to alveoli, across to blood, onto hemoglobin, through the left heart, out the arteries, into the muscle capillaries, and finally into the muscle fiber where it fuels energy release. Carbon dioxide travels the reverse route to the lungs. A common check during training is the talk test: if you can speak in short phrases but not sing, your breathing and heart rate suggest a moderate effort. Measuring heart rate during and after exercise, then timing how fast it returns toward rest, gives a simple window on fitness."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Write the formula for cardiac output. 2) Which side of the heart sends blood to the lungs? 3) What molecule carries most of the oxygen in the blood? 4) Why can a trained athlete have a low resting heart rate yet a normal cardiac output? 5) Where in the lungs does gas exchange happen? 6) If heart rate is 150 and stroke volume is 100 milliliters, what is cardiac output? Answers: 1) Cardiac output equals heart rate times stroke volume. 2) The right side of the heart pumps oxygen-poor blood to the lungs. 3) Hemoglobin inside red blood cells carries most of the oxygen. 4) A strong heart has a large stroke volume, so it can pump the same amount per minute with fewer beats. 5) Gas exchange happens in the alveoli, the tiny air sacs where oxygen and carbon dioxide cross thin walls. 6) 150 times 100 equals 15,000 milliliters, or about 15 liters a minute."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: confusing arteries and veins by direction. Arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins return it, regardless of whether the blood is oxygen-rich; the vessels to the lungs are the exception to the usual oxygen pattern. Mistake: thinking a low resting heart rate means a weak heart. In endurance athletes it usually signals a strong heart with a large stroke volume. Mistake: forgetting that cardiac output depends on both numbers, so raising only heart rate tells half the story. Exit ticket: (a) What two quantities multiply to give cardiac output? (b) What happens to breathing rate and depth when you begin to exercise, and why? Answers: (a) Heart rate and stroke volume multiply to give cardiac output. (b) Breathing becomes faster and deeper to bring in more oxygen and remove more carbon dioxide, matching the higher demand of working muscles."}]}'::jsonb, 'Arteries always carry blood away from the heart and veins return it, no matter whether the blood is oxygen-rich.', 14),
  (4, 'Energy systems: how the body fuels exercise', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to name the three energy systems the body uses to fuel exercise and match each one to the kind of effort it powers. You will explain that every muscle contraction spends a molecule called ATP, and that the three systems are simply different ways to rebuild ATP at different speeds. You will describe the ATP-PC system for very short, explosive efforts, anaerobic glycolysis for hard efforts lasting up to a couple of minutes, and the aerobic system for long, steady work. You will also be able to explain why a sprinter and a distance runner draw on these systems in very different proportions. Warm-up from the diagnostic: a weightlifter makes one maximal lift lasting about two seconds, while a runner jogs steadily for forty minutes. Which athlete relies mostly on stored fuel used without oxygen, and which depends on a continuous oxygen supply to keep rebuilding ATP?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Muscles run on a molecule called ATP, short for adenosine triphosphate. ATP stores a small amount of ready energy, and a muscle holds only a few seconds worth, so the body must rebuild it constantly. There are three systems for this. The ATP-PC system uses a stored compound called phosphocreatine to recharge ATP almost instantly; it needs no oxygen but runs out in about ten seconds, so it powers jumps, throws, and short sprints. Anaerobic glycolysis breaks down carbohydrate without oxygen to make ATP quickly; it can fuel hard efforts up to about one or two minutes, but it produces hydrogen ions that build up and contribute to the burning feeling and fatigue. The aerobic system uses oxygen to break carbohydrate and fat down fully into carbon dioxide and water; it makes far more ATP per unit of fuel and can run for hours, but it delivers energy slowly, so it suits endurance work. The three systems always work together, blending across any activity. Which one dominates depends on how hard and how long the effort lasts. As one system fades, the next takes over, which is why pace management matters so much in sport."},{"heading":"Key methods & examples","text":"Match the effort to the system by asking how hard and how long. Example: a shot put or a single heavy lift lasts one to three seconds, so the ATP-PC system dominates. Example: a 400 meter run takes under a minute of all-out effort, so anaerobic glycolysis supplies most of the ATP and the athlete finishes with heavy legs from accumulated hydrogen ions. Example: a half marathon lasts over an hour at a steady pace, so the aerobic system supplies almost all the energy, burning a mix of carbohydrate and fat. The systems overlap in time. In a soccer match a player jogs aerobically for most of the game, then calls on the ATP-PC system for a sudden sprint and on glycolysis for a sustained chase, recovering aerobically between bursts. Recovery itself is aerobic: oxygen taken in after hard effort helps rebuild phosphocreatine and clear the byproducts of glycolysis. A practical takeaway is that training should rehearse the system a sport relies on, so a sprinter trains short maximal bursts while a distance runner logs long steady miles to build aerobic capacity."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What molecule do all three energy systems rebuild? 2) Which system powers a two second maximal lift? 3) Roughly how long can the ATP-PC system last at full effort? 4) Which system produces hydrogen ions that contribute to fatigue? 5) Which system fuels a one hour steady run? 6) Why does recovery between sprints rely on oxygen? Answers: 1) All three rebuild ATP, the molecule a muscle spends to contract. 2) The ATP-PC system, using stored phosphocreatine, powers very short maximal efforts. 3) About ten seconds before phosphocreatine stores run low. 4) Anaerobic glycolysis makes ATP quickly but releases hydrogen ions that build up. 5) The aerobic system, which uses oxygen to release energy slowly over a long time. 6) Oxygen is needed to rebuild phosphocreatine and clear the byproducts of glycolysis, which is an aerobic process."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating the three systems as separate switches rather than overlapping contributors. They always blend; one simply dominates depending on intensity and duration. Mistake: blaming lactic acid alone for muscle burn. The modern view is that accumulating hydrogen ions, along with other factors, drives the burning sensation and fatigue, and lactate itself is reused as fuel. Mistake: thinking the aerobic system helps only in long events. It is slower to deliver energy, but it is central to recovery between efforts of every kind. Exit ticket: (a) Order the three systems from the one that acts fastest to the one that sustains effort longest. (b) Which system would a 100 meter sprinter rely on most? Answers: (a) ATP-PC acts fastest, anaerobic glycolysis next, and the aerobic system sustains the longest efforts. (b) A 100 meter sprinter relies mostly on the ATP-PC system, with glycolysis adding support."}]}'::jsonb, 'Intensity and duration together decide which energy system dominates - ask how hard and how long.', 13),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"When you bend the elbow to lift a weight, the muscle that shortens as the agonist is the...","options":["triceps","biceps","hamstring","diaphragm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The biceps shortens to flex the elbow, making it the agonist; the triceps is the antagonist."},{"prompt":"Cardiac output is calculated as...","options":["heart rate plus stroke volume","heart rate times stroke volume","stroke volume minus heart rate","heart rate divided by stroke volume"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cardiac output equals heart rate multiplied by stroke volume, the blood pumped per minute."},{"prompt":"Most oxygen is carried in the blood by...","options":["plasma alone","hemoglobin in red blood cells","white blood cells","platelets"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hemoglobin inside red blood cells binds and carries the large majority of the oxygen."},{"prompt":"The ATP-PC energy system can power maximal effort for about...","options":["ten seconds","two minutes","twenty minutes","two hours"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Phosphocreatine stores recharge ATP for only about ten seconds of all-out effort."},{"prompt":"A hard 400 meter run relies mainly on...","options":["the ATP-PC system","anaerobic glycolysis","the aerobic system only","no energy system"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An all-out effort under a minute depends mostly on anaerobic glycolysis, leaving heavy legs."},{"prompt":"Gas exchange between air and blood takes place in the...","options":["alveoli","arteries","tendons","ventricles"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The alveoli are the thin-walled air sacs where oxygen and carbon dioxide cross into and out of the blood."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'Principles of training and fitness components', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"After this lesson you will be able to state the main principles of training - overload, specificity, progression, and reversibility - and use them to judge whether a training plan will actually improve fitness. You will also be able to list the main components of fitness, such as aerobic endurance, muscular strength, muscular endurance, speed, flexibility, and body composition, and give a simple test for each. You will understand the idea of the FITT variables - frequency, intensity, time, and type - as the dials you adjust to apply overload safely. Warm-up from the diagnostic: a runner jogs the same easy three miles every day for a year and is puzzled that she is no faster. Using the words overload and progression, explain why her fitness has stopped improving and what single change to her training would start it moving again."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Training works by stress and recovery. When you place a greater demand on the body than it is used to, it adapts and becomes stronger during the rest that follows. That greater demand is overload, and it is the core principle. Specificity says the body adapts to the exact demand you place on it, so training must match the goal: lifting heavy builds strength, long steady runs build aerobic endurance. Progression says you must keep increasing the demand gradually as you adapt, or improvement stalls. Reversibility is the flip side: stop training and the gains fade, often faster than they were built. You apply overload through the FITT variables. Frequency is how often you train, intensity is how hard, time is how long each session lasts, and type is the kind of activity. Adjusting any of these raises the demand. Fitness itself is not one thing. Aerobic endurance is the ability to sustain effort using oxygen; muscular strength is the greatest force a muscle can produce; muscular endurance is repeating force many times; speed, flexibility, and body composition round out the common list. A good plan names the component it targets and then overloads it specifically and progressively while allowing recovery."},{"heading":"Key methods & examples","text":"To build a session, pick the component, then set the FITT variables to overload it. Example: to improve aerobic endurance, you might run three times a week at a moderate pace for thirty minutes, then progress by adding five minutes every two weeks or lifting the pace slightly. Example: to build strength, you lift a weight heavy enough that you can manage only six to eight repetitions, and you progress by adding load as it starts to feel easy. Specificity guides the choice of type: a swimmer trains mostly by swimming, because adaptations in the exact muscles and movement patterns transfer best. Common fitness tests make progress measurable. A timed run estimates aerobic endurance, a one repetition maximum lift estimates strength, a maximum number of push-ups estimates muscular endurance, and a sit-and-reach test estimates flexibility. Reversibility explains why a plan must be sustainable: two weeks off through illness can undo recent gains, so consistency beats occasional heroics. The runner from the warm-up improves the moment she adds overload, for example by lengthening her runs, raising her pace, or adding a hill session, and then progressing from there."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which principle says the body adapts to the specific demand placed on it? 2) What do the letters in FITT stand for? 3) Name the principle that explains why fitness fades when you stop training. 4) Give a simple test for muscular endurance. 5) To keep improving, what must you do to the training demand over time? 6) Which component does a heavy six repetition lift mainly develop? Answers: 1) Specificity, the principle that adaptations match the exact training demand. 2) Frequency, intensity, time, and type. 3) Reversibility, which says gains are lost when training stops. 4) A maximum number of push-ups or sit-ups in good form tests muscular endurance. 5) You must gradually increase it, which is progression, so overload continues. 6) Muscular strength, since a near-maximal load for few repetitions targets maximum force."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: doing the same workout forever and expecting more fitness. Without added overload and progression, the body has no reason to keep adapting. Mistake: training in a way that does not match the goal, such as only jogging to prepare for a strength event, which ignores specificity. Mistake: pushing overload too fast, which raises injury risk and skips the recovery that lets adaptation happen. Exit ticket: (a) State the difference between overload and progression. (b) Which FITT variable are you changing when you run at a faster pace rather than for longer? Answers: (a) Overload is placing a greater than normal demand on the body so it adapts, while progression is gradually increasing that demand over weeks as the body gets used to it. (b) You are changing intensity, since pace reflects how hard the effort is, whereas running longer would change time."}]}'::jsonb, 'Overload is the greater-than-normal demand; progression is increasing that demand gradually over time.', 13),
  (7, 'Nutrition for performance and energy balance', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"After this lesson you will be able to describe the main nutrients an athlete needs, explain energy balance, and match food choices to the demands of training and competition. You will be able to say what carbohydrates, fats, and proteins each do for performance, why hydration matters, and how the timing of meals around exercise can help. You will understand energy balance as the relationship between the energy you take in from food and the energy you spend, and you will see how a surplus, a deficit, or a balance changes body mass over time. Warm-up from the diagnostic: two athletes eat the same meals, but one trains two hours a day and the other barely moves. If both want to keep a steady body mass, which one needs to eat more, and what does that tell you about matching food intake to energy expenditure?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Food supplies both energy and the materials the body needs to work and repair. Carbohydrate is the main fuel for moderate and hard exercise; the body stores it as glycogen in muscle and liver, and running low on glycogen is a common cause of fatigue in long events. Fat is a large, slow-burning fuel used heavily during low intensity work and at rest. Protein mainly supplies the building blocks for repairing and growing muscle after training, rather than acting as a primary fuel. Vitamins and minerals support countless processes, and water keeps temperature, blood volume, and joint function in order; even mild dehydration can lower performance. Energy balance is the simple accounting of energy in against energy out. Energy in comes from food and drink. Energy out is the sum of resting needs, the cost of digesting food, and the energy spent in activity. When intake equals expenditure, body mass holds steady. A lasting surplus is stored and mass rises; a lasting deficit draws on stores and mass falls. Athletes adjust intake to their training load and their goal, eating more carbohydrate on hard days and ensuring enough protein spread through the day to support recovery."},{"heading":"Key methods & examples","text":"Plan nutrition around the training demand. Example: before a long endurance session, an athlete eats a carbohydrate-rich meal a few hours ahead to top up glycogen, then drinks water to start well hydrated. Example: during events lasting beyond about an hour, taking in some carbohydrate, often as a sports drink, helps hold pace as glycogen falls. Example: after hard training, a mix of carbohydrate to refill glycogen and protein to support muscle repair aids recovery. Energy balance guides body composition goals. An athlete wanting to add muscle pairs strength training with a modest energy surplus and enough protein; one wanting to reduce body fat uses a modest deficit while keeping protein high to protect muscle. Hydration has a practical check: pale urine usually signals good hydration, while dark urine suggests the athlete should drink more. A rough way to estimate energy out is to add resting needs to the energy cost of the day''s activity; the more and harder you train, the larger that second part grows. Matching intake to that total is what keeps mass and performance steady, which answers the warm-up: the athlete who trains more must eat more to stay balanced."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which nutrient is the main fuel for hard exercise? 2) In what form does the body store carbohydrate? 3) What is the main job of dietary protein for an athlete? 4) Define energy balance in one sentence. 5) If energy intake stays below energy expenditure for weeks, what happens to body mass? 6) Give a simple everyday sign of good hydration. Answers: 1) Carbohydrate is the main fuel for moderate and hard exercise. 2) The body stores carbohydrate as glycogen in the muscles and liver. 3) Protein supplies the building blocks for repairing and building muscle after training. 4) Energy balance is the comparison between energy taken in from food and energy spent by the body. 5) Body mass falls, because the body draws on its stored energy to make up the deficit. 6) Pale rather than dark urine is a simple sign of being well hydrated."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating protein as the main fuel for exercise. Protein mostly repairs and builds tissue; carbohydrate and fat are the main fuels. Mistake: ignoring hydration until thirsty, when performance may already be dropping; drinking steadily is better. Mistake: assuming a large energy deficit is always good for an athlete, when too big a deficit can cost muscle, energy, and health. Exit ticket: (a) What is stored as glycogen, and why does running low on it cause fatigue in long events? (b) If an athlete eats more energy than they spend for a long period, what happens and why? Answers: (a) Carbohydrate is stored as glycogen, the muscle''s ready fuel for hard work; when it runs low the muscles lose their main energy source and tire. (b) Body mass rises, because the extra energy the body does not spend is stored, much of it as fat."}]}'::jsonb, 'Carbohydrate and fat are fuels; protein is mainly for repair - keep the roles separate.', 13),
  (8, 'Biomechanics, skill acquisition, and motor learning', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to explain a few basic ideas from biomechanics - force, levers, balance, and how technique uses them - and describe how people learn and refine motor skills. You will be able to analyze a simple movement in terms of the forces acting on the body and the levers the skeleton forms, and you will understand why good technique often means using larger levers and the whole body rather than one joint. On the skill side, you will be able to name the stages a learner passes through, distinguish practice types, and explain how feedback shapes improvement. Warm-up from the diagnostic: a beginner throwing a ball uses only the arm, while an expert steps, rotates the hips and trunk, then whips the arm last. In terms of adding more body segments and longer levers, why does the expert throw so much faster?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Biomechanics applies the physics of forces and motion to the body. A force is a push or pull; in sport the main forces are muscle force, gravity, the ground pushing back when you push down on it, and resistance from air or water. The skeleton forms levers, and using a longer lever lets the end move faster, which is why a throw gains speed when the arm extends and the body adds rotation. Balance depends on the base of support and the center of mass: keeping the center of mass over a wide base makes a position stable, while moving it toward the edge makes movement easier to start. Good technique sequences forces from large body parts to small, transferring momentum from legs to trunk to arm. Skill acquisition is how these movements are learned. Learners commonly pass through a first stage of thinking hard about every part, a second stage of smoother practice with fewer errors, and a final stage where the skill runs almost automatically and attention is free for tactics. Skills can be mostly closed, performed in a stable setting like a free throw, or open, needing constant adjustment like a pass in a fast game. Feedback and well-designed practice drive the whole process."},{"heading":"Key methods & examples","text":"To analyze a movement biomechanically, label the forces and the levers. Example: in a standing jump, the legs push down on the ground, and by Newton''s idea of equal and opposite forces the ground pushes the body up; bending and then extending the hips, knees, and ankles in sequence stacks their contributions. Example: a longer implement such as a hockey stick or a tennis racket extends the lever so the end moves fast, trading some control for speed. Balance examples are everywhere: a sprinter in the blocks sets a low center of mass ready to drive forward, while a gymnast widens the base of support to hold a steady landing. For learning skills, practice design matters. Blocked practice repeats one skill many times and suits early learning, while varied practice mixes skills and better prepares a performer for open, changing situations. Feedback can come from the performer''s own senses or from a coach, and feedback that is clear and not too frequent helps the learner build their own sense of a good movement. Breaking a complex skill into parts can help a beginner, while practicing the whole skill suits movements that flow as one unit, such as a golf swing."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name the force that pushes the body up when you push down on the ground. 2) Why does a longer lever help a throw or a strike? 3) What two things decide how stable a body position is? 4) In which learning stage does a skill feel almost automatic? 5) Give an example of a closed skill. 6) When is blocked, repetitive practice most useful? Answers: 1) The ground reaction force, the equal and opposite push from the ground, drives the body up. 2) A longer lever makes its end move faster for the same rotation, adding speed to the throw or strike. 3) The size of the base of support and the position of the center of mass over that base. 4) In the final, autonomous stage, when the skill runs with little conscious thought. 5) A free throw or a golf putt, performed in a stable, predictable setting, is a closed skill. 6) Blocked practice is most useful early, when a learner is first grooving a new movement."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking more muscle force alone makes a faster throw. Technique that sequences the body and uses longer levers often matters more than raw strength. Mistake: giving a learner constant, detailed feedback on every attempt, which can stop them building their own feel for the movement. Mistake: using only blocked practice right up to competition, when varied practice better prepares a performer for changing, open situations. Exit ticket: (a) Explain why an expert thrower who steps and rotates the whole body throws faster than a beginner who uses only the arm. (b) What is the difference between an open skill and a closed skill? Answers: (a) The expert adds more body segments and longer, faster-moving levers and transfers momentum from legs to trunk to arm, so the hand and ball reach a much higher speed. (b) An open skill is performed in a changing environment and needs constant adjustment, while a closed skill is performed in a stable, predictable setting."}]}'::jsonb, 'A longer lever moves its end faster, so technique that adds body segments beats arm-only effort.', 14),
  (9, 'Key terms', 'vocab', '{"items":[{"term":"agonist","translation":"the muscle that shortens to drive a movement, also called the prime mover","example":"In an elbow curl the biceps is the agonist while the triceps relaxes as the antagonist."},{"term":"cardiac output","translation":"the volume of blood the heart pumps each minute, equal to heart rate times stroke volume","example":"A heart beating 70 times a minute at 70 milliliters per beat gives a cardiac output of about 4.9 liters a minute."},{"term":"ATP","translation":"adenosine triphosphate, the molecule that stores and releases the energy a muscle uses to contract","example":"Every energy system in the body works by rebuilding ATP as fast as the muscle spends it."},{"term":"overload","translation":"placing a greater demand on the body than it is used to, so that it adapts and improves","example":"Adding weight to the bar applies overload to the working muscles."},{"term":"glycogen","translation":"the stored form of carbohydrate held in muscle and liver and used as fuel during exercise","example":"Running low on glycogen is a common cause of fatigue late in a marathon."},{"term":"ground reaction force","translation":"the equal and opposite push the ground gives back when you push down on it","example":"A jumper drives down into the floor and the ground reaction force lifts the body up."},{"term":"VO2 max","translation":"the greatest rate at which a person can take in and use oxygen, a key measure of aerobic fitness","example":"Months of endurance training can raise an athlete''s VO2 max."},{"term":"doping","translation":"the banned use of drugs or methods to gain an unfair performance advantage in sport","example":"Anti-doping tests protect fair play and the health of athletes."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (10, 'Health, recovery, injury prevention, and fair play', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"After this final lesson you will be able to explain why regular physical activity is good for health, describe the risks of a mostly sedentary lifestyle, and outline how athletes recover well, avoid injury, and compete fairly. You will be able to list several health benefits of activity across the heart, muscles, bones, and mind, and you will understand why long periods of sitting carry their own risks even for people who exercise. You will also be able to describe simple recovery and injury-prevention methods and explain why fair play and anti-doping rules protect both health and the meaning of sport. Warm-up from the diagnostic: a person runs hard for forty minutes in the morning, then sits almost all day at a desk. Can regular exercise fully cancel the risks of sitting for many hours, or do the two act somewhat separately on health?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Regular physical activity brings wide-ranging benefits. It strengthens the heart and improves the delivery of oxygen, lowers the risk of heart disease and type 2 diabetes, helps control body mass, builds muscle and bone, and supports mood, sleep, and clearer thinking. A mostly sedentary lifestyle, built on long hours of sitting and little movement, raises the risk of these same conditions. Importantly, long sitting carries risk somewhat separately from exercise, so breaking up sitting with short movement helps even active people. Recovery is the part of training where adaptation actually happens. Good recovery includes enough sleep, sensible spacing of hard sessions, refueling with food and fluid, and lighter active recovery days. Injury prevention rests on a few reliable ideas: warming up before effort, increasing training load gradually rather than in sudden jumps, using sound technique, balancing opposing muscle groups, and resting when pain signals a problem. Finally, sport depends on fair play. Doping, the banned use of drugs or methods to gain an advantage, is tested for and punished because it threatens athletes'' health and the honesty of competition. Fair play also means respecting rules, opponents, and officials. Taken together, these ideas connect exercise to a long, healthy, and meaningful active life."},{"heading":"Key methods & examples","text":"Turn these ideas into habits. Example: public health advice suggests adults aim for about 150 minutes of moderate activity each week plus some strengthening work, which can be split into short sessions. Example: to break up sitting, a desk worker stands or walks for a few minutes every half hour, which reduces the separate risk of long sitting. For recovery, an athlete after a hard session sleeps well, eats carbohydrate and protein, hydrates, and schedules an easy day before the next hard one. For injury prevention, a runner raising weekly distance follows a gradual increase rather than a sudden jump, warms up with easy movement, and stops if a sharp pain appears, following the simple rest, ice, compression, and elevation approach for a minor soft-tissue injury. For fair play, athletes check that any supplement is from a trusted source, since contaminated products can cause a failed test, and they respect officials'' decisions even in a tense match. A useful mindset is that the goal of training is long-term improvement and health, so protecting the body and competing honestly matter more than any single result won by cutting corners."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name three health benefits of regular physical activity. 2) Why can long sitting be a risk even for someone who exercises daily? 3) During which part of the training process does adaptation actually occur? 4) Give two methods that help prevent injury. 5) What does doping mean, and why is it banned? 6) What simple approach suits a minor soft-tissue injury? Answers: 1) Any three of: a stronger heart, lower risk of heart disease and type 2 diabetes, better body-mass control, stronger muscles and bones, and improved mood and sleep. 2) Long sitting carries risk somewhat separately from exercise, so hours of being still are not fully cancelled by one workout. 3) During recovery, when rest, sleep, and refueling let the body adapt to the training stress. 4) Any two of: warming up, increasing load gradually, good technique, and resting when pain appears. 5) Doping is the banned use of drugs or methods to gain an unfair advantage; it is banned to protect health and fair competition. 6) Rest, ice, compression, and elevation."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: believing a single daily workout cancels all the harm of sitting for many hours. Activity and prolonged sitting affect health somewhat separately, so both regular exercise and frequent movement breaks matter. Mistake: skipping recovery and training hard every day, which blocks the adaptation that rest allows and raises injury risk. Mistake: increasing training load in sudden large jumps, a common path to overuse injury. Exit ticket: (a) Explain why recovery, not just the workout itself, is essential for improvement. (b) Give one reason anti-doping rules exist. Answers: (a) The body adapts and grows stronger during recovery, when rest, sleep, and refueling repair the stress of training; without it the body cannot consolidate gains and may break down. (b) Anti-doping rules protect athletes'' health and keep competition fair and honest, so results reflect genuine ability rather than banned substances."}]}'::jsonb, 'Exercise and prolonged sitting affect health somewhat separately, so do both: move often and train regularly.', 12)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0004-0000-4000-8000-000000000037' and l.position = v.pos
);
